---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { meta } from '../lib/build-data';
const m = meta();
---

<Base title="About: how mod readiness is worked out" description="How the tracker decides whether a mod is ready, where the data comes from, and how your privacy is protected.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      This site answers one question every time Minecraft ships a new version: <em>are my mods ready yet?</em> It tracks the
      top {m.tracked.fabric} Fabric and {m.tracked.neoforge} NeoForge mods on Modrinth and refreshes automatically.
    </p>

    <h2>What the statuses mean</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>✓ Ready</strong>: the author has published a <em>release</em> build declared compatible with that version (any patch, e.g. 26.1.2 counts for 26.1).</li>
      <li><strong>β Beta only</strong>: only beta or alpha builds are declared.</li>
      <li><strong>◐ Snapshot build</strong>: for upcoming versions, a build exists for a snapshot, pre-release or release candidate.</li>
      <li><strong>✕ Not yet</strong>: the mod supports this loader but nothing is declared for this version.</li>
      <li><strong>⊘ Inactive</strong>: no updates for over a year and not on recent versions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      All of this is <strong>author-declared</strong>. A mod can work without being tagged, or be tagged and still have bugs. Treat
      "ready" as "declared compatible", and back up your world before upgrading.
    </p>

    <h2>Server, client or both</h2>
    <p>
      Each mod says on Modrinth whether it's needed on the client, the server, or both. Your list is <em>server ready</em> when every
      mod the server needs is ready, and likewise for the client. Mods marked optional on both sides (common for libraries and
      utilities) count on both, because if they're in your list you're running them; we flag them so you know you could leave
      them off one side. If a mod doesn't say, we assume it's needed on both.
    </p>

    <h2>Alternatives</h2>
    <p>
      For mods that aren't ready, we suggest a few ready mods with similar descriptions and categories, plus a short hand-checked
      list of known replacements. Suggestions are a starting point, not an equivalent: check features before switching.
      Libraries have no alternatives because other mods depend on them by name.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      No accounts, no cookies, no ad trackers. Your lists are stored in your own browser. Share links carry the list in the part
      of the URL after <code>#</code>, which browsers never send to our server. We use cookieless, aggregate page-view counts only.
      Mod icons load from Modrinth's CDN.
    </p>

    <h2>Credits</h2>
    <p>
      Mod data from <a href="https://modrinth.com" rel="noopener">Modrinth</a>; version data from Mojang's public version manifest.
      Not an official Minecraft product. Not approved by or associated with Mojang or Microsoft.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: 720px; }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
</style>
